Planning a yoga retreat always starts with the same question: where? The venue does half the work. You need calm, space, light, a place where guests feel cut off from everyday life the moment they arrive. The Château de la Huberdière, in Touraine, hosts yoga retreats and wellness courses on a fourteen-hectare estate hired on an exclusive-use basis, walled grounds included.

Here is what we have learned from hosting these stays, and what really matters to an organiser.

## Choosing a venue for a yoga retreat

A good yoga retreat needs three things: a generous practice space, comfortable rooms, and silence. The rest is comfort. La Huberdière brings these three conditions together in one place, which avoids travel and keeps the group together from morning to evening.

The estate is hired exclusively. You are not in a hotel shared with other guests: the château, its sitting rooms and its grounds are yours for the length of the course. This exclusivity changes everything for a group's focus. The details on capacity and set-up are on the [retreat](/en/yoga-retreat-venue) page.

## A quiet château in the heart of Touraine

The château sits in walled, wooded grounds at Nazelles-Négron, near Amboise. You hear the wind in the trees, not the traffic. It is this silence that lets a meditation practice or a morning session take its full place.

Touraine adds its mild climate and its Loire Valley landscapes. Between sessions, guests walk in the grounds, read in the sun, or head to the nearby banks of the Loire. This natural setting is part of the care: it soothes before the first posture.

## The practice space: the Orangery and the grounds

The heart of a retreat is the space where you practise. The Orangery offers 200 sqm bathed in light, fully adaptable: empty for one mat per person in yoga, set with tables for a writing workshop. The four more intimate sitting rooms host sub-groups, one-to-one talks or quiet time.

Once the fine weather arrives, the grounds become the studio: sunrise yoga on the lawn, talking circles under the trees, meditative walks across the fourteen hectares. You adapt the layout to your discipline, and we prepare the rooms accordingly.

## Accommodation and catering for the group

For a course, logistics make the difference between a smooth retreat and a tiring one. The château sleeps up to 20 guests in ten suites, each with a private bathroom, in single or shared occupancy depending on the spirit of the stay.

Catering matters as much as yoga on a retreat. I prepare seasonal cooking, adaptable to vegetarian or gluten-free diets, using produce from the market and the kitchen garden. The details of the table are on the [dining](/en/chateau-dining-touraine) page. Sleeping, practising and eating in the same place keeps the group together: the rhythm of the course holds firm, from waking to the last session.

## Beyond yoga: meditation, writing, personal development

The venue lends itself to all gentle practices, not only yoga. Meditation and mindfulness find rare ground in the calm of the estate. Writing residencies and workshops make the most of the Orangery and the hushed sitting rooms. Personal development seminars, digital detox retreats and creative courses, whether painting, theatre or music master classes, all have their place here.

This versatility draws a varied set of organisers, and several return year after year. That is the best sign that a venue has found its place in a retreat calendar.

## Access for guests

An out-of-the-way venue should not be hard to reach. The château is about 2 hrs 20 from Paris by road, or one hour by high-speed train to Saint-Pierre-des-Corps then thirty minutes by car. Far enough for a change of scene, close enough that guests come without losing a day. We help organise arrivals and, if needed, shuttles from the station.

## What format and length for a retreat

The format is chosen according to your audience and your intention. The weekend, from Friday evening to Sunday, suits introductory retreats and groups travelling far for a few days: two nights, four to five sessions, a real break. The full week allows deeper work, with a teaching thread from Monday to Sunday and days that alternate practice, rest and nature.

Between the two, a long weekend of three nights is often the best compromise: long enough to settle into a rhythm, short enough to stay accessible. As for group size, ten to fifteen guests keep an intimate dynamic, up to twenty for a more intensive retreat. We adapt the accommodation, the meals and the rooms to each of these set-ups.

## Planning your retreat

Whether you are preparing a weekend or a week, we build the welcome around your programme: spaces, rooms, meals, schedules. You keep control of the content, we handle the rest.

To check our availability and receive a proposal, visit the [retreat](/en/yoga-retreat-venue) page or write to us. We reply within 24 hours.
